  "worked_solution": "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=2.$ Substituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=6.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =4{c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{3}+4{c}_{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=6$ and ${c}_{1}=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =4(2)+{c}_{2} \\\\ 6 & =8+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=2{x}^{4}-2{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.\n$$",
